had a productive discussion with the  trade unions yesterday the key focus of  the meeting was to look at next year's  pay review body and the evidence that we  submit to that we want to work  constructively with the unions in terms  of the evidence that is submitted there  and obviously building on what has come  out from the OVR and what is Affordable  to the wide economy but of course as  part of that meeting we also listened to  the trade unions in terms of their  concerns in respect of this year that's  part of a wider discussion that is  happening across government where other  departments other secretaries of state  are meeting with their trade unions and  of course I will take those messages  back from the health unions in terms of  my discussions with the chancellor  for the right process is to look at next  year's independent pay-per-view body we  believe as a government that The  Independents or the pay review Body  Matters and that is the right way  forward indeed the opposition have said  that they themselves support the  pay-per-view body process so it's right  that we use that as the the main  mechanism and we work together with the  trade unions to recognize the cost of  living pressures that there are on staff  across the economy but including within  the NHS so we want to get the evidence  into the pay review body right and work  constructively on that but of course as  part of that discussion the trade unions  also emphasize that for them this year  was also important we're having a  conversation across government with  trade unions because obviously this  impacts our number of departments and I  will take those messages back in my  discussions with the chancellor  the Prime Minister the government's been  consistent throughout that we have an  independent pay-per-view body uh process  and that is the right way forward and  we've been consistent on that and that  is why uh given not least that we're  more than three quarters away through we  don't think it would be appropriate to  go back to last April to retrospectively  go back all those months in terms of  what has already happened this year we  had a process that looked at the issues  in the round but at the same time you  want to listen to the trade unions want  to engage with them constructively you  want to get the right evidence into the  pay-per-view body next year and of  course they made further representations  in terms of this year notwithstanding  that we're more than three quarters of  the way through the year and it is right  that we listen to those concerns  what I'm saying is we're particularly  focused on the pay-per-view body which  will start from a thankful that is the  right approach and we've been consistent  on that but at the same time we're  engaging we're listening we're working  with Trade union colleagues because we  recognize there's very real pressure on  the health system at the moment and as  part of that obviously it is not helpful  to have the strikes tomorrow that is a  concern in terms of ambulances a key  part of my visit today is to see the  Fantastic work that the team are doing  here at London Central and West they  take over 600 000 111 calls a year they  do a formidable job in terms of dealing  with urgent calls of course if people  have an urgent call issue it is  important that they call 111 if they  have an emergency then of course 999 but  there will be pressure on the system  tomorrow so it's important that people  are bearing that in mind in terms of  calling 111 if it's an urgent matter do  you want to on the level of anger among  health workers on the suggested that  they should work harder for any pain  well that wasn't said in the meeting and  the Trade union leader that made those  comments outside the Departments of  Health bizarrely wasn't actually in the  meeting on which he was commenting and I  saw he did a media round again this  morning talking about a meeting that he  wasn't actually at but there is common  ground with the trade unions in terms of  how do we ensure that clinicians who are  highly trained are focused on clinical  work and not having to divert time on  Administration on some of the  frustrating tasks that they have to do  how do we get the right Tech Support to  them how do we work better across the  system as a whole so I think there's an  important difference between working  better working in ways across the whole  NHS family that ensure patients are  treated in the right place at the right  time and that maximizes the clinical  skills particularly of our nursing staff  and that is something that the train  engine themselves welcome in terms of  ensuring that some of the things that  are very frustrating for staff around  for example technology we work in a  better way on that that's very different  to the comments that were made by  someone who wasn't actually in the room  about a meeting they hadn't actually  attended  well we recognized in our I gave a  statement to the House of Commons  yesterday recognizing they're very  severe pressure particularly emergency  departments have faced of course the  significant pressure as a consequence of  flu we've seen a seven-fold increase in  the number of flu cases compared to last  month a hundred times the number of  people in hospital with flu compared to  last year and that of course comes on  top of a high number of missions from  covid and also a backlog in terms of  delayed discharge as a consequence of  the pandemic and a further challenge of  the spiking flu that we've faced is that  also has an impact in terms of Supply  because stuff absence is also higher  when you have those flu spikes so we've  had a number of factors come together  that's created very significant pressure  that's why in the House of Commons  yesterday I announced a further 250  million pounds of funding that we've  re-prioritized within our budget  responding to what I have heard over  Christmas  in terms of the pre